  "worked_solution": "To find which two consecutive whole numbers $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between, we first identify the perfect squares that are just below and just above $110.$\n\nThe perfect squares closest to $110$ are $100$ and $121.$ We have\n\n$$\n100<110<121.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of each number, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{100} & <\\sqrt{110}<\\sqrt{121} \\\\ 10 & <\\sqrt{110}<11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14185481/assets/9bef06cb0000173c30147f4f9f633f3adec207b688adb005bcf0fff1aa8d7b13.png)\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{110}$ lies between the consecutive whole numbers $10$ and $11.$ \n\nTherefore, since $a<b,$ we have $a=10$ and $b=11.$",
